# ADR-<NNNN>: <the decision in one line, present tense>

Author: <who> · Task: <#N> · Date: <YYYY-MM-DD> · Updated: <YYYY-MM-DD>

Copy to `docs/adr/<NNNN>-<short-slug>.md`. Four sections, none extra: a long ADR
is not written while designing but added afterwards from memory — and then it repeats what is already
visible in the code. The circumstances of the case live inside "Why", not in a separate section.

## Decision

What was decided — a list, present tense, no justification. Readable in ten seconds by someone
who came to learn "how we do it here".

## Why

The measurement, constraint or cost that makes the decision what it is. Also here — the circumstances
in which the choice was made: what was in the system, what got in the way, what was feared. Reasoning without a fact ("it is
cleaner") means the decision was not chosen but just happened.

## Alternatives

Each one a line: what was considered and why it did not fit. An empty section is more honest than an invented one: it says
alternatives were not sought, and the next reader will see that.

## Risks

What we pay: what becomes more expensive, slower or more fragile; under what condition the decision must be reversed.
An empty section = risks were not sought — that is also information, and it should not be hidden.

---

When an ADR is NOT created: the decision is reversible and cheap; the subject is taste, not a choice with a cost; the rule is already
recorded where a machine reads it (linter config, import contract, gate step). The record is kept
AS the decision is made: an ADR added afterwards from memory repeats what is visible in the code, and an empty
"Alternatives" section is more honest than an invented one.
